 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(d \log d)$ due to the sorting step required for rank transformation, where $d$ is the feature dimension. The subsequent Pearson correlation is $O(d)$.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(d)$ to store the rank vectors.
-*   **Strengths:** Robust to non-linear monotonic relationships. Does not assume linearity or normality of the data distribution. Effective when the relationship between features is consistent in order but not in magnitude.
-*   **Limitations:** Less powerful than Pearson correlation if the relationship is truly linear and the data is normally distributed. Sensitive to ties. Does not capture non-monotonic relationships (e.g., U-shaped).
-*   **When to use:** When the feature dimensions have a monotonic but potentially non-linear relationship, or when the data contains outliers that would distort linear correlation.
-*   **When not to use:** When the relationship is known to be linear and Gaussian, or when non-monotonic relationships are of interest.
