 ## Background
-Spearman correlation is a rank-based correlation coefficient introduced by Charles Spearman. In the context of volume rendering and 3D scene understanding, it is used as a metric to probe vision models by comparing rendered features against target features. It is distinct from cosine similarity (which measures direction) and Pearson correlation (which measures linear co-variation) [S1, unofficial]. It probes a stricter notion of equivalence than cosine, as a reconstruction can align in aggregate direction (high cosine) yet have poorly coordinated feature dimensions (low Pearson) [S1, unofficial]. The gap between Pearson and Spearman correlation can indicate whether a mismatch is driven by outlier dimensions or systematic non-linear warps [S1, unofficial].
